issues.body:
### What happened?

With stdin kept open, an idle `--mode rpc` process stays alive after an extension calls `ctx.shutdown()` asynchronously, for example from a timer callback. The shutdown request is not processed until another RPC command arrives.

The [RPC shutdown handler](https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/f09d01b85e0384647fcab6779d35ab9dabe04be3/packages/coding-agent/src/modes/rpc/rpc-mode.ts#L345-L347) only sets `shutdownRequested`; it checks the flag after stdin commands or `agent_settled`. An idle request has neither trigger.

### Steps to reproduce

Save `shutdown-probe.mjs` in an empty directory:

```js
export default function (pi) {
  pi.on("session_start", (_, ctx) => {
    setTimeout(() => {
      process.stderr.write("shutdown requested\n");
      ctx.shutdown();
    }, 200);
  });
  pi.on("session_shutdown", () => process.stderr.write("session_shutdown\n"));
}
```

Run with stdin left open:

```sh
PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR="$(mktemp -d)" pi --mode rpc --no-session -ne -e ./shutdown-probe.mjs
```

After `shutdown requested`, the process remains alive and `session_shutdown` is not emitted. Enter `{"type":"get_state"}` followed by Enter: the query succeeds, `session_shutdown` is emitted, and Pi exits with code 0. Reproduced with isolated configuration and only the probe extension enabled.

### Expected behavior

An idle `ctx.shutdown()` request should perform orderly shutdown without requiring another stdin command.

### Version

Pi 1.4.0; Node.js 22.23.2; macOS 27.2 arm64. The same handler is present at commit `f09d01b8`.

### What happened?

While a manual compaction (`/compact` or `session.compact()`) is running, an extension calling `pi.sendMessage(msg, { triggerTurn: true })` starts a new run right away. The run executes concurrently with the compaction: its request uses the uncompacted context, and its messages end up before the compaction entry.

In 1.2.0, `sendCustomMessage` only checks `isStreaming` (`_isAgentRunActive`) before calling `_startRun`; it doesn't check `isCompacting`. `ctx.isIdle()` does return false at that point, so the two disagree. Since `compact()` begins with `abort()`, I assume compaction isn't meant to overlap with a run.

If possible, I'd be happy to help fix this.


### Steps to reproduce

1. Load this extension:

```ts
export default function (pi) {
  pi.on("session_before_compact", async () => {
    pi.sendMessage({ customType: "demo", content: "hello", display: false }, { triggerTurn: true });
    await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 100));
  });
}
```

2. Have a session with some history and run `/compact`.
3. Event order: `compaction_start` → `session_before_compact` → `agent_start` → … → `agent_settled` → `compaction_end`. Branch tail: `[message, custom_message, message, compaction]`.


### Expected behavior

The message is queued while compaction runs and the run starts after `compaction_end`, the same way it waits while a run is active.


### Version

1.2.0 (same in 1.1.0)

**Version:** pi-coding-agent 1.4.0, Anthropic `claude-opus-5-5` (`supportsMidConvoSystemMessages`, `supportsMidConvoToolChanges`)

### What happens

`emitContext` passes `context` handlers the conversation without system messages. When a handler returns anything different (detected by comparing message references), `restoreSystemMessages` collapses every system message into one leading message holding the current prompt sections and tools.

This also happens when the handler only replaces the content of an old message and keeps every message where it was. If the session already has a mid-conversation system message (a tool added, a section updated), that update moves into the head, so the start of the request changes.

### Verified without a provider

Transcript: system, user, assistant with a thinking block, system adding a `subagent` tool, user. The handler replaces the assistant message with a copy whose thinking text has a prefix removed (new object, same position, array spliced in place).

- No-op handler: roles `system,user,assistant,system,user`, leading tools `read,bash`
- Content-only handler: roles `system,user,assistant,user`, leading tools `read,bash,subagent`, different leading system message

<details><summary>Script</summary>

```js
// Run with Node from a Pi 1.4.0 install; adjust PI to the pi-coding-agent package folder.
const PI = "<path>/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";
const { ExtensionRunner } = await import(`file:///${PI}/dist/core/extensions/runner.js`);
const { getInitialSystemMessage, getCurrentTools } = await import(`file:///${PI}/node_modules/@earendil-works/pi-ai/dist/utils/transcript.js`);

const tool = (name) => ({ name, description: name, parameters: { type: "object", properties: {} } });
const transcript = [
  { role: "system", content: "Base prompt", toolsAdded: [tool("read"), tool("bash")], timestamp: 1 },
  { role: "user", content: "hello", timestamp: 2 },
  { role: "assistant", content: [{ type: "thinking", thinking: "Thinking: reasoning", thinkingSignature: "sig" }, { type: "text", text: "hi" }], timestamp: 3 },
  { role: "system", content: "", sections: { tools: "<tools>read, bash, subagent</tools>" }, toolsAdded: [tool("subagent")], timestamp: 4 },
  { role: "user", content: "next", timestamp: 5 },
];
function stripPrefix(event) {
  const next = event.messages.map((m) => m.role !== "assistant" ? m : {
    ...m, content: m.content.map((b) => b.type === "thinking" ? { ...b, thinking: b.thinking.replace("Thinking: ", "") } : b),
  });
  event.messages.splice(0, event.messages.length, ...next);
}
async function run(handler) {
  const runner = new ExtensionRunner([{ path: "synthetic", handlers: new Map([["context", [handler]]]) }], {}, process.cwd());
  const out = await runner.emitContext(transcript);
  return { roles: out.map((m) => m.role).join(","), leadingTools: getCurrentTools([getInitialSystemMessage(out)]).map((t) => t.name).join(",") };
}
console.log("no-op:", await run(() => {}));
console.log("content-only:", await run(stripPrefix));
```
</details>

### Observed in a real session

pi-tool-display edits old thinking blocks this way on every request (MasuRii/pi-tool-display#33). In one session, enabling a tool mid-conversation was followed by cache reads dropping from 56k to 19.8k tokens and 43k tokens written again, and Anthropic reported `thinking_dropped` with `prefix_binding_mismatch`. That is an observation, not a controlled billing test.

### Expected

A content-only edit that keeps the conversation's messages should not move system updates to the head. Telling a content edit apart from a structural change (reordering two messages of the same role, removing one and adding another) probably needs reliable mapping to the original messages or an explicit API, so I'm not proposing a specific algorithm.

Regression cases worth covering: editing one message keeps system messages in place; reordering messages of the same role still behaves safely.

Related: #11157 (another path to the same collapse).

### What happened?

`ctx.isProjectTrusted()` returns `true` for a project with an explicit "Do not trust" decision saved in `~/.pi/agent/trust.json`, as long as that project has no trust-requiring resources.

`main.ts:743-750`:

https://github.com/earendil-works/pi/blob/381a2d8eb62b9f7e4c710cf66aa516f8a92cb9bd/packages/coding-agent/src/main.ts#L743-L750

With no trust-requiring resources, `!hasTrustRequiringResources` short-circuits the `||` and the store is never consulted, so a saved `false` is ignored.

this is the api extensions were given to honor trust decisions (#5523) — and the user's no never reaches them sometime: after declining a repo, one of my extensions stripped its agents.md from the system prompt, while another, asking `isprojecttrusted()`, got `true` and injected the same agents.md right back.

### Steps to reproduce

1. `mkdir /tmp/trust-bug && printf '# untrusted\n' > /tmp/trust-bug/AGENTS.md` — no `.pi/`, nothing trust-requiring.
2. Open pi there and decline trust (`/trust` → "Do not trust"). Confirm `~/.pi/agent/trust.json` now has `"/tmp/trust-bug": false`.
3. Reopen pi with a session_start extension logging `ctx.isProjectTrusted()` — it reports `true`, even though the entry in `~/.pi/agent/trust.json` says `false`.

<details>
<summary>Probe extension used for the measurements</summary>

Drop this in `~/.pi/agent/extensions/` — it logs one JSON line per session start:

```ts
import { appendFileSync } from "node:fs";
import { homedir } from "node:os";
import { dirname, resolve } from "node:path";
import { ProjectTrustStore, type ExtensionAPI } from "@earendil-works/pi-coding-agent";

const LOG_PATH = "/tmp/pi-trust-probe.log";
const agentDir = resolve(homedir(), ".pi", "agent");
const trustStore = new ProjectTrustStore(agentDir);

// Nearest non-null decision for cwd or an ancestor — how /trust resolves a path.
function savedDecision(cwd: string): boolean | null {
    let dir = resolve(cwd);
    for (;;) {
        const decision = trustStore.get(dir);
        if (decision !== null) return decision;
        const parent = dirname(dir);
        if (parent === dir) return null;
        dir = parent;
    }
}

export default function trustProbe(pi: ExtensionAPI): void {
    pi.on("session_start", (event, ctx) => {
        if (event.reason === "reload") return;
        appendFileSync(
            LOG_PATH,
            `${JSON.stringify({
                time: new Date().toISOString(),
                cwd: ctx.cwd,
                hasUI: ctx.hasUI,
                isProjectTrusted: ctx.isProjectTrusted(),
                savedDecision: savedDecision(ctx.cwd),
            })}\n`,
        );
    });
}
```

</details>

Measured with that probe (`saved decision` = the entry pi's `/trust` wrote to `~/.pi/agent/trust.json`; `null` = no entry for the project or any ancestor):

| project | saved decision | `isProjectTrusted()` |
|---|---|---|
| AGENTS.md only | **false** | **true** |
| `.pi/settings.json` present | false | false |
| README.md only | null | true |

### Expected behavior

A saved decision should be authoritative regardless of trust-requiring resources: consult the trust store before falling back to the resource-less auto-trust. 
I'm happy to submit a PR for that. 

### Workaround
Consumers can read `ProjectTrustStore` directly — that's what my extensions do.

### Version

1.1.0

**What happened?**

An overlay does not cover a terminal image. If a transcript row holds a Kitty or iTerm2 image and an overlay sits on top of it, the image stays visible and the overlay simply has no rows there: no panel background, no text, a hole exactly the size of the image.

It is deliberate-looking code in `packages/tui/src/tui.ts`:

```ts
export function compositeTuiLine(baseLine, overlayLine, startCol, overlayWidth, totalWidth) {
  if (isImageLine(baseLine))
    return baseLine;
```

so any image row inside the overlay rectangle is passed through untouched.

**Steps to reproduce**

No terminal needed:

```js
import { compositeTuiLine, encodeKitty, allocateImageId } from "@earendil-works/pi-tui";

const png = "iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAAEAAAABCAYAAAAfFcSJAAAADUlEQVR42mP8z8BQDwAEhQGAhKmMIQAAAABJRU5ErkJggg==";
const row = encodeKitty(png, { columns: 20, rows: 5, imageId: allocateImageId() }) + " ".repeat(20);
const panel = "OVERLAY PANEL ROW".padEnd(40, " ");

compositeTuiLine(row, panel, 30, 40, 100) === row; // true, overlay dropped
```

In pi: open a session where an image was rendered (read a PNG), then open any overlay that covers it (`ui.custom(..., { overlay: true })`). The image is drawn over the panel.

**Expected behavior**

The overlay wins inside its own rectangle. Either delete the Kitty placements that intersect the overlay bounds and re-emit the image when the overlay closes, or at minimum let the panel's lines replace the covered rows.

**Why it matters**

A bordered panel with an opaque background is the normal way to show a picker or dialog. With an image above it, the panel gets a translucent-looking hole and the user reads the image through the UI. Sizing or background changes in the extension cannot fix it, since the row never reaches compositing.

**Version**

@earendil-works/pi-tui 1.4.0 (kitty and iTerm2 protocols; `compositeTuiLine` behaves the same for both).

I can implement this if it is wanted: track image placements per row, and on overlay render/close delete and re-emit the intersecting ones.

### Problem

The Dream Diary (`DREAMS.md`) output is always in English because the diary-generation prompt is hardcoded inside the `memory-core` plugin. Users whose workspace language is non-English (e.g., Chinese) end up with a mixed-language workspace — all other files are in Chinese but Dream Diary entries are in English.

This is not just a cosmetic issue: it breaks workspace language consistency and requires manual post-editing after every dreaming sweep.

### Proposal

Add one of these config options under `plugins.entries.memory-core.config.dreaming`:

1. **`dreaming.language`** — simple locale/language hint (e.g., `"zh-CN"`, `"ja"`) that the diary prompt uses to steer output language.
2. **`dreaming.diaryPrompt`** — full custom prompt override for maximum flexibility (language, style, format, length).

Option 1 is the common case; option 2 is the power-user escape hatch. Both could coexist — if `diaryPrompt` is set, it takes precedence over `language`.

### Example

```jsonc
{
  "plugins": {
    "entries": {
      "memory-core": {
        "config": {
          "dreaming": {
            "enabled": true,
            "language": "zh-CN"
            // or:
            // "diaryPrompt": "用中文写一段简短的梦境日记，风格简洁，不超过三段。"
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

### Environment

- OpenClaw version: latest
- OS: WSL2 (Linux)

### Bug type

Behavior bug (incorrect output/state without crash)

### Beta release blocker

No

### Summary

Discord's native `/status` fails with `SessionWorkStartInvalidatedError: Session account selection changed while preparing authentication. Retry.` when the direct status path passes a cloned session entry into a model-selection guard that requires object identity with the session store. This reproduces with unchanged state and a valid synthetic credential; no concurrent account change is needed.

### Steps to reproduce

Observed live workflow:

1. In a Discord conversation whose existing OpenClaw session has a saved `authProfileOverride` (the affected session has `authProfileOverrideSource: "auto"`), invoke the registered native `/status` command.
2. Observe `Command failed. Check the Gateway logs for details.`
3. Repeat the command. The affected operator reports reliable failure; the exact exception was logged four times between 19:05:33 and 19:08:32 UTC+03:00 on October 11.

A self-contained isolated reproducer is included below. It creates synthetic session state through the normal SQLite session accessor and calls the actual direct-status core helper. It does not require a Discord bot, real credentials, or concurrent writes.

### Expected behavior

Return the session's status without falsely reporting that unchanged account selection changed. The [documented command](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/ebdc7d54c0842d96c4bb2fb2bb4be4758f07f255/docs/tools/slash-commands.md#L298-L305) shows execution/runtime status, uptime, plugin health and provider usage/quota.

The read-only loader intentionally returns a separate cloned entry; an [existing loader test](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/ebdc7d54c0842d96c4bb2fb2bb4be4758f07f255/src/gateway/session-utils.test.ts#L1714-L1763) explicitly expects `loaded.entry !== loaded.store[parentKey]`. A read-only status consumer must compose correctly with that contract and must not change the saved account merely to display status.

### Actual behavior

The direct status helper throws before rendering. Discord substitutes the generic command-failure notice. A saved profile activates the failing branch even when the profile exists and is eligible.

The isolated test shows:

- **Valid saved profile:** two fresh direct-status calls throw the exact error. Renderer is never reached. Stored session remains unchanged.
- **Missing automatic saved profile:** the same two-call rejection and unchanged stored state.
- **No saved profile:** the same read-only loader still produces distinct objects, but direct status reaches the renderer.
- **Causal control:** with the valid profile and exactly the same entry values, supplying a map whose value is the same entry object makes the real model-selection routine succeed without changing the entry.

### OpenClaw version

Reproduced on source/build `2026.9.9`, commit `ebdc7d54c0842d96c4bb2fb2bb4be4758f07f255`. The production build's compiled caller, clone and guard were also inspected and match this mechanism.

At the follow-up check on October 11, upstream `main` was `a45edbfa69ea3f299e02d31f57cd0755a92af640`. These three source files were each byte-identical to the tested checkout:

- `src/plugin-sdk/command-status.runtime.ts`
- `src/gateway/session-utils-store.ts`
- `src/auto-reply/reply/model-selection.ts`

That is a source comparison, not a claimed test run on the newer commit.

### Operating system

Ubuntu 24.04.5 LTS; Node v26.11.1; Python 3.12.3 for the portable runner.

### Install method

Source checkout with dependencies installed; Gateway launched from built `dist/index.js`. The reproduction runs the repository's own Vitest runner against source.

### Model

No inference is needed to trigger the failure. The isolated fixture selects `openai/gpt-4o` and uses a synthetic API-key-shaped auth profile.

### Provider / routing chain

Discord native command -> direct status SDK helper -> read-only session loader -> model selection -> local exception before status rendering. The reproduction makes no Discord or model-provider request.

### Additional provider/model setup details

The live target session has a saved automatic auth-profile selection. Its private profile identifier is omitted. The test proves that unavailable credentials are not a prerequisite: both valid and missing synthetic profiles fail.

### Logs

Observed Gateway entries (UTC+03:00; no private channel/session/profile identifiers included):

```text
2026-10-11T19:05:33.111+03:00 error discord/monitor discord interaction handler failed: SessionWorkStartInvalidatedError: Session account selection changed while preparing authentication. Retry.
2026-10-11T19:07:29.521+03:00 error discord/monitor discord interaction handler failed: SessionWorkStartInvalidatedError: Session account selection changed while preparing authentication. Retry.
2026-10-11T19:07:45.364+03:00 error discord/monitor discord interaction handler failed: SessionWorkStartInvalidatedError: Session account selection changed while preparing authentication. Retry.
2026-10-11T19:08:32.896+03:00 error discord/monitor discord interaction handler failed: SessionWorkStartInvalidatedError: Session account selection changed while preparing authentication. Retry.
```

The production log does not contain a full stack or command arguments for these entries. The operator's native-command observation, matching saved session condition, inspected deployed code and isolated real-helper reproduction establish the reported path; no instrumented live Discord replay is claimed.

### Root cause and source trail

1. [Discord's direct-status branch](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/ebdc7d54c0842d96c4bb2fb2bb4be4758f07f255/extensions/discord/src/monitor/native-command.ts#L586-L610) invokes [`maybeDeliverDiscordDirectStatus`](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/ebdc7d54c0842d96c4bb2fb2bb4be4758f07f255/extensions/discord/src/monitor/native-command-status.ts#L34-L47) for the target conversation session.
2. [`resolveDirectStatusReplyForSessionCore`](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/ebdc7d54c0842d96c4bb2fb2bb4be4758f07f255/src/plugin-sdk/command-status.runtime.ts#L45-L85) calls the read-only loader, then passes `statusLoaded.entry` and `statusLoaded.store` together into `createModelSelectionState`.
3. [`loadSessionEntryWithMode`](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/ebdc7d54c0842d96c4bb2fb2bb4be4758f07f255/src/gateway/session-utils-store.ts#L155-L180) retains the original entry in the returned store but sets the returned `entry` to `structuredClone(canonicalMatch.entry)`.
4. [Stored-auth handling](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/ebdc7d54c0842d96c4bb2fb2bb4be4758f07f255/src/auto-reply/reply/model-selection.ts#L461-L467) runs when there is an auth-profile override. The [current-selection guard](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/ebdc7d54c0842d96c4bb2fb2bb4be4758f07f255/src/auto-reply/reply/model-selection.ts#L503-L533) includes:

```ts
sessionStore[sessionKey] !== sessionEntry
```

This is already true because of step 3, before any concurrent change. The guard is invoked even when the credential is present and eligible. Every fresh retry recreates the same mismatch.

5. The exception escapes the status helper. Discord's [interaction failure notice](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/ebdc7d54c0842d96c4bb2fb2bb4be4758f07f255/extensions/discord/src/internal/interaction-dispatch.ts#L103-L127) and [monitor logger](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/ebdc7d54c0842d96c4bb2fb2bb4be4758f07f255/extensions/discord/src/monitor/listeners.ts#L91-L102) produce the observed user/log symptoms.

History attributes the identity guard to [af55058459c / #166985](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/166985), which protects selected accounts across awaited credential preparation. The separate loader cloning predates that guard. This is source-history attribution, not a full release bisection or a claim of a tested last-known-good version.

### Runnable diagnostic and verified results

Requirements: the tested OpenClaw checkout with its normal dependencies installed, supported Node, and Python 3. Save the complete script below as `reproduce.py`, then run:

```sh
python3 reproduce.py /path/to/openclaw
```

The runner generates an external temporary Vitest test/config, uses the checkout's normal test setup and real SQLite session accessor, and cleans its owned temporary state. It does not edit production source or session data.

**Test boundary:** the direct-status helper, read-only loader, model-selection routine and SQLite session state are real. Provider auth/catalog IO and the final status renderer are fixtures; unrelated background maintenance is disabled. The success control proves reaching the renderer, not final formatting or Discord delivery.

**PASS means the current defect and causal controls were reproduced, not that the product is fixed.** Both failing-profile cases explicitly expect the error twice. A repair regression should invert those expectations to successful status resolution while retaining the no-mutation assertion and genuine execution-invalidation coverage.

