## What Problem This Solves

Fixes: exec allowlist checks could treat a shell command as safe even when a standalone assignment performs recursive arithmetic, parameter, subscript, or keyed compound-array evaluation before an allowlisted command runs.

## User Impact

User impact: recursively evaluated standalone assignments now fail closed instead of executing silently. Ordinary scalar assignments, unkeyed arrays, and command-prefixed environment assignments keep their existing behavior. Keyed compound-array assignments now require approval because their key semantics depend on prior shell array state.

## Why This Change Was Made

The shell command explainer now marks recursively evaluated standalone assignments as unanalyzable, so the existing authorization policy fails closed. This includes keyed compound-array entries that tree-sitter flattens and cannot safely classify using prior indexed-versus-associative array state. Assignment preludes also recognize `NAME+=value command` consistently with `NAME=value command`. Existing shell-text decoding and risk classification were moved into focused sibling modules to keep the owning files within the repository line-cap policy.

## Evidence

- Exact head: `befe832957da3f58ae0bb6a7eb7f8d925d94c532`.
- Negative cases: scalar arithmetic indirection, subscripted assignment names, direct keyed arrays, keyed-array entry appends, outer append keyed arrays, and numeric keyed arrays produce a `dynamic-assignment` risk and require approval.
- Positive controls: literal and quoted scalar assignments and unkeyed arrays remain analyzable, while `NAME=value command` and `NAME+=value command` remain environment preludes.
- Final-effect proof: the shipped node-host `system.run` entry point launched `STATIC=value; /usr/bin/echo static-ok` once and returned `static-ok`. It rejected all three recursive forms before the process runner, with zero launches and no marker files.
- Parent baselines: the scalar gateway regression failed on the original parent because the command completed; the compound parser and gateway regressions failed before their respective compound-array remediations for the same reason.
- `pnpm test src/infra/command-explainer/extract.test.ts src/infra/command-explainer/format.test.ts src/infra/exec-authorization-allowlist.test.ts src/infra/exec-authorization-plan.test.ts src/infra/exec-authorization-render.test.ts src/infra/exec-safe-builtins.test.ts src/infra/exec-approvals-allow-always.test.ts src/infra/exec-approvals-safe-bins.test.ts src/node-host/invoke-system-run-allowlist.test.ts src/agents/bash-tools.exec-host-gateway.test.ts src/agents/bash-tools.exec-host-node.test.ts src/agents/bash-tools.exec-authorization.integration.test.ts --maxWorkers=1`: 12 files passed, 292 tests passed, 3 skipped because optional local shells were unavailable.
- Focused `oxfmt`, focused `oxlint --openclaw-focused-config`, line-cap and test-line ratchets, `tsgo:core`, `tsgo:test:src`, and `git diff --check` passed. CI identified two stale `max-lines` directives and their corresponding baseline entries after the refactor shortened both files; those four stale records were removed, focused lint passed, and the max-lines plus line-cap ratchets passed at this head.

